---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/brevo-vs-mailchimp';
const title = 'Brevo vs Mailchimp for Shopify SMS Marketing (2026)';
const desc = 'Compare Brevo and Mailchimp for Shopify launch calendars, eligible SMS, contact and CRM limits, permission, inventory and purchase exits.';
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "When does Brevo fit?",
    "answer": "Evaluate Brevo for a team seeking email, contact management and SMS campaigns together. Confirm contact storage, automation capacity, sales CRM package and SMS destination requirements on the purchased plan."
  },
  {
    "question": "When does Mailchimp fit?",
    "answer": "Mailchimp can fit an established audience CRM and ecommerce email operation. Marketing SMS needs an eligible paid plan, approved application, supported sending market and purchased credits; email access alone does not enable it."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does a Shopify order authorize promotional SMS?",
    "answer": "No. Keep channel and purpose permissions separate, with source, timestamp and disclosure evidence. Transactional permission and a synced phone number do not establish promotional permission."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should purchase exits be tested?",
    "answer": "Place an order while a message is queued, then replay a delayed event. Verify the customer exits the promotion before dispatch and receives no duplicate after retries. Pause affected sends if the suppression feed is stale."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should we add another email platform?",
    "answer": "Only for a documented workflow gap. Both candidates offer email; another sender adds identity, consent and suppression coordination work. Assign one owner to each message job before considering Sequenzy or a dedicated SMS service."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which costs less?",
    "answer": "Obtain quotes for the same eligible contacts, destination mix, email volume and SMS segments. Include plan limits, credits, carrier fees, CRM add-ons and integration work. There is no universal cost winner."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a> / Brevo vs Mailchimp</nav>
      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="badge">Campaign marketing comparison</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Brevo vs Mailchimp for Shopify SMS marketing</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Choose around your actual launch calendar, supported destinations and customer data. Both offer email and SMS capabilities; neither creates permission or inventory truth merely by importing a Shopify profile.</p>
        <p class="mt-5">The merchant names below are illustrative planning scenarios retained from this comparison, not verified customers or measured results.</p>
      </header>
      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox title="Conditional fits at a glance" picks={[
          { label: 'Brevo', tool: 'Brevo', reason: 'Consider for coordinated email, contact management and SMS when plan capacity and destination requirements fit.' },
          { label: 'Mailchimp', tool: 'Mailchimp', reason: 'Consider for an existing audience CRM and ecommerce email team when eligible paid SMS fits its markets.' },
          { label: 'Ownership', tool: 'Your team', reason: 'Keep one sender per job and prove consent, stock checks and purchase exits before release.' },
        ]} />
      </div>
      <div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto">
        <h2 class="px-5 py-4 font-serif text-2xl font-black">Comparison at a glance</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Brevo</th><th>Mailchimp</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Email and contacts</td><td>Check both send allowance and stored-contact tier; do not assume unlimited contacts.</td><td>Check audience billing, contact status and email allowance for the chosen plan.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Marketing SMS</td><td>Confirm credits, destination coverage, sender registration and country restrictions.</td><td>Eligible paid plan, approval, credits and supported sending market required.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>CRM and workflows</td><td>Contact management is not every sales CRM package; verify automation capacity and actions.</td><td>Audience CRM is not a guarantee of every ecommerce trigger or journey action; verify plan access.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Shopify launch control</td><td>Prove mapped events, stock freshness and exits through the installed integration.</td><td>Prove mapped events, stock freshness and exits through the installed integration.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Verify channels and entitlements before designing a calendar</h2>
        <p>Brevo is a candidate when a team wants to coordinate email and SMS with contact management. Its marketing tiers have send and contact limits; sales CRM packages and automation capacity require separate review. Mailchimp is a candidate when the team already operates its audience CRM and ecommerce email workflows. Its marketing SMS is a paid add-on with application approval and country-specific registration. Confirm the exact triggers, actions, seats and contact counts your account can use before committing either workflow.</p>
        <p>Country coverage is not interchangeable with approval to use a sender. Check the business location, recipient destination, sender type, permitted content and opt-out mechanism for each market. Keep unsupported destinations out of the SMS queue rather than borrowing another country's registration.</p>
        <p>Current references: <a class="link" href="https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/208589409-About-Brevo-s-pricing-plans">Brevo plan scope</a>, <a class="link" href="https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/208717449-Supported-countries-and-pricing-for-SMS-messages">Brevo SMS destinations and costs</a>, and <a class="link" href="https://mailchimp.com/help/about-sms-marketing/">Mailchimp SMS eligibility and availability</a>. Recheck these against the actual account before purchase.</p>
        <h2>Shopify data authority and permission</h2>
        <p>Use Shopify order IDs, customer IDs and variant inventory as commerce references, and document which integration supplies each field and its delay. Keep the consent ledger authoritative for channel, purpose, capture source, time and disclosure version. A browser event, imported email address or checkout phone is not proof of promotional SMS consent.</p>
        <p>Normalize phone numbers and map Shopify IDs to provider profiles without blindly merging shared household numbers or changed email addresses. Resolve conflicting permissions conservatively. Preserve channel-specific opt-outs and broader do-not-contact requests during import; never turn a transactional contact into a marketing subscriber through a status overwrite.</p>
        <h2>Launch and inventory planning examples</h2>
        <p>Cipher Audio could send a product-story teaser email before a headphone launch, reserving a no-discount SMS for consented, engaged browsers. Measure whether the extra channel adds margin rather than treating a launch as proof of lift.</p>
        <p>Sauce Lab could use batch-number scarcity only after checking remaining bottle stock at dispatch. Cancel a queued scarcity reminder if the inventory feed is stale or the batch sells out.</p>
        <p>Ember Ceramics could separate the maker story email from waitlist access to a small kiln release. Preserve queue order and fulfillment capacity; a large waitlist does not justify notifying everyone at once.</p>
        <p>Loom &amp; Frame could disclose edition size and offer repeat collectors early access. Define the order-history segment in Shopify, then test whether a later sell-through reminder reaches only people who have not purchased.</p>
        <p>Driftwood Surf Co could align boardshort education with swell content and send restock notices to the matching waitlist. Compare eligible waitlist outreach with a holdout instead of asserting that it beats a full-list announcement.</p>
        <p>SoleDrop ATX could stage sneaker access by waitlist priority and available size. Recheck variant stock before general email release so an early-access queue cannot produce sold-out links for everyone else.</p>
        <h2>Calendar coordination and offer discipline</h2>
        <p>Noir Collective could schedule VIP streetwear access before the general drop. Put both channels on one calendar and test a lower frequency against comparable eligible recipients; do not assume reduced frequency raises revenue per send.</p>
        <p>Festival Threads Co could route tour-merch campaigns by city and venue timezone. Apply recipient-local sending policy and a shared frequency budget, including rescheduled shows and travelers whose location is uncertain.</p>
        <p>Vinyl Vault Records could disclose pressing quantity and prioritize the waitlist and repeat collectors. Define collector value from reconciled net orders, with refunds handled, rather than treating a raw lifetime-spend field as permission.</p>
        <p>Bloom &amp; Wick could plan holiday teasers and shipping deadlines ahead of peak demand, separating gift bundles from self-purchase scent education. Use declared preferences without inferring permission from a gift recipient's delivery details.</p>
        <p>Peak Trail Gear could reserve clearance offers for a bargain-sensitive segment and compare with a holdout. Keep full-price loyalists separate to test whether an incentive adds sales or merely discounts demand that already exists.</p>
        <p>Ridge Run Coffee could exclude active subscribers from acquisition-style flash offers. Assign a fresh subscription-status source and stop the campaign when that exclusion cannot be evaluated.</p>
        <p>Patchwork Denim could match one-of-one listings to stated style preferences. Cancel queued messages once the item sells and prevent a retried listing event from restarting the same promotion.</p>
        <p>Arena Energy could keep a shaker offer in email and use SMS for athlete-collaboration drop timing. Coordinate the promises across channels so recipients do not receive conflicting eligibility or duplicate incentives.</p>
        <h2>Proposed release checks, not executed results</h2>
        <p>For each candidate, queue a promotion and then place an order, revoke SMS permission, exhaust a variant and delay the event feed. Confirm purchase exits, purpose-specific suppression and inventory cancellation before dispatch. Test duplicate and out-of-order events with stable event IDs, an order/flow deduplication key and a documented replay window. A retry must not re-enroll a buyer or revive an old opt-in.</p>
        <p>Name an owner for Shopify mapping, permission records, campaign approval and failed-sync response. Define a freshness threshold, a pause control and a reconciliation report. During migration, choose a cutover owner, preserve suppressions, disable the old trigger and reconcile queued messages before enabling the replacement. Review <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent basics</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign restraint</a>; confirm destination-specific requirements with qualified advice rather than relying on a universal quiet-hours rule.</p>
        <h2>Pricing and incremental margin</h2>
        <p>Quote both stacks for the same eligible contacts, peak email volume, SMS destinations and encoded message segments. Include storage and automation limits, SMS credits, carrier charges, CRM packages, seats, integration maintenance and migration work. Preview actual text encoding and concatenation costs; character count alone does not establish the billed segment count.</p>
        <p>For an offer test, compare net sales after discounts and returns per eligible customer between comparable treatment and holdout groups. Subtract product, fulfillment, messaging and support costs once; discounts already included in net sales must not be deducted again. Include incremental platform costs when assessing the decision. Set the observation window around the purchase and return cycle and enough eligible volume, rather than promising a fixed pilot duration or treating attributed revenue as incremental lift.</p>
        <h2>Choose the owner of each message job</h2>
        <p>Choose Brevo if its account scope and country coverage fit the coordinated campaigns your team can operate. Choose Mailchimp if its audience workflows and eligible SMS support the same verified requirements with manageable operating cost. Either decision depends on the release checks above.</p>
        <p>Both candidates already offer email. Consider Sequenzy only for a specific unmet lifecycle workflow, and a dedicated SMS provider only for a demonstrated sending requirement. Assign one tool to each welcome, drop, education or winback job; do not use a second email platform as ungoverned overflow. No catalog ranking determines this comparison.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Brevo vs Mailchimp FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
